Let us start by addressing the differences between $\Delta G_R$ and $\Delta G^{\circ}_R$, and how these properties relate to equilibrium. $\Delta G_R(P, T)$ depends on pressure, whereas $\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$ depends only on temperature. The value of $\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$  is directly related to the pressure ratio between reactants and products. Exergonic reactions at standard conditions ($\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$<0) will have larger product pressures than reactant pressures. This is because a negative $\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$ will give $K_c$ values larger than one. Differently, endergonic reactions at standard conditions ($\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$>0)  will have smaller product pressures than reactant pressures. This is because a positive $\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$ will give $K_c$ values smaller than one.
